module gunbc.floor_attempt_standing

import std.types { String }
import v2.std.logic { Bool }

// HEAD-STANDING AND LANE-TERMINALITY ARE TWO INDEPENDENT AXES, AND EVERY SHAPE THIS GATE HAS
// WORN SO FAR COLLAPSED THEM INTO ONE.
//
// The required `witnesses` aggregator answers one question -- may this head merge -- and it has
// been answering it from one input, the two lane results. That input cannot distinguish an
// attempt whose head is still the pull request's head from an attempt whose head was replaced
// while it ran, so the two previous shapes each picked a side and each is fail-open somewhere:
//
//   `always()`      -- the job runs after a workflow cancellation, `cancelled != success` fires,
//                      and a lawfully superseded push-over-push publishes a FAILED aggregate over
//                      a head whose work has intentionally moved on. Fail-open on the READER: it
//                      spends investigation on abandoned heads, which it did, repeatedly.
//   `!cancelled()`  -- the job does not run at all when the workflow was cancelled, so the modal
//                      supersession is quiet. But cancellation does not prove supersession: a
//                      manual cancel, a runner eviction, a workflow timeout or an operator
//                      stopping a queue create no replacement head, and the LIVE head is then
//                      left with no verdict. Fail-open on the MERGE CANDIDATE, which is worse.
//
// Neither is a wrong answer to its own question. They are answers to two different questions,
// and the defect is that one condition was being asked to carry both. This module separates
// them: `FloorHeadStanding` is what the head axis observes, `FloorLaneTerminal` is what the lane
// axis observes, and `floor_rendered_verdict` is the one total composition over the pair. The
// composed rule the operator ruling fixes, and which neither previous shape can express:
//
//   cancelled (or skipped, or abandoned) AND measured != current  -> superseded, do not investigate
//   cancelled (or skipped, or abandoned) AND measured == current  -> evidence UNESTABLISHED, BLOCK
//
// `cancelled -> benign` and `cancelled -> failed` are both forbidden as universal readings.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE IS NOT. It is not a merge-admission receipt and it is not the stable-head
// lease that would let the expensive floor fold run only for the exact head it was admitted for.
// Those are separate concepts the same ruling names and tells us to keep separate; fusing the
// names here is how a lease becomes an admission by accident.

// THE ATTEMPT'S OWN IDENTITY, carried as the wire strings the workflow context renders.
//
// EVERY FIELD IS A STRING AND NONE IS OPTIONAL, and that is deliberate rather than lazy. These
// are values GitHub interpolates into a shell step; an absent context renders as the EMPTY
// STRING and never as a missing key, so `String` with an empty inhabitant is what the boundary
// actually produces. Modelling them as `String?` would invent a distinction the transport cannot
// carry and would let a reader believe an absence had been observed as such.
//
// `pull_request` is empty for push, merge_group and workflow_dispatch runs. That emptiness is
// the whole content of `NoPullRequestSubject` below -- an attempt with no pull request cannot be
// superseded by a newer push to one.
//
// THERE IS NO `run_attempt` FIELD, AND ITS ABSENCE IS A DECISION RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.
// `run_attempt` has no shape anywhere in `extdeps/` -- it exists in this repository only as the
// raw env-var name `GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT` read by `tools.merge_admission_current_context`. The head
// axis does not need it: head-standing is decided by measured head against current head, and
// nothing yet needs to distinguish two attempts of the SAME run. Adding the field now would be a
// carrier with no consumer, which is the thing this module is otherwise arguing against.
type FloorAttemptSubject {
  pull_request: String
  measured_head: String
  run_id: String
}

// THE RECEIPT LABELS, DECLARED ONCE AND CONSUMED TWICE.
//
// `gunbc.required_lanes_gate` renders this line in shell, interpolating workflow contexts it
// cannot evaluate here; this module renders it from a value. Two realizations of one line, so
// the labels are one authority rather than two spellings, and a witness joins the emitted yaml
// to `floor_attempt_subject_receipt` over a fixture subject.
data floor_attempt_receipt_prefix: String = "required floor attempt: pull_request="
data floor_attempt_receipt_measured_head_label: String = " measured_head="
data floor_attempt_receipt_run_id_label: String = " run_id="
data floor_attempt_receipt_standing_label: String = " head_standing="

fn floor_attempt_subject_receipt(subject: FloorAttemptSubject, standing: FloorHeadStanding) -> String {
  join(
    [
      floor_attempt_receipt_prefix,
      subject.pull_request,
      floor_attempt_receipt_measured_head_label,
      subject.measured_head,
      floor_attempt_receipt_run_id_label,
      subject.run_id,
      floor_attempt_receipt_standing_label,
      floor_head_standing_name(standing: standing)
    ],
    ""
  )
}

// WHY THE HEAD AXIS CAN FAIL TO ANSWER, split by who owns the repair.
//
// NoPullRequestSubject is NOT a deficit. The attempt has no pull request, so no newer push can
// replace its head and the head axis is inapplicable rather than unmeasured. It composes exactly
// as `FloorHeadCurrent` does, and the two are kept distinct only so the receipt can say which
// one it was.
//
// CurrentHeadNotAcquired IS a deficit, and it is the one arm that must never be read as benign:
// not knowing whether this attempt was superseded is not evidence that it was.
type FloorHeadUnobservableCause =
    NoPullRequestSubject
  | CurrentHeadNotAcquired { detail: String }

// THE HEAD AXIS. `FloorHeadSuperseded` is the only state that can excuse a stopped lane, and it
// is reachable only from TWO POSITIVE OBSERVATIONS that disagree -- never from one observation
// plus an assumption about the other.
type FloorHeadStanding =
    FloorHeadCurrent { measured: String }
  | FloorHeadSuperseded { measured: String, current: String }
  | FloorHeadUnobservable { measured: String, cause: FloorHeadUnobservableCause }

// THE LANE AXIS, decoded from `needs.<job>.result`.
//
// `LaneAbandoned` is on this list because a production run put it there. Run 32883390033 read
// `BUILD="cancelled" FLOOR="abandoned"`; `abandoned` is a `needs.*.result` value nobody here knew
// was reachable. THE RESIDUAL ARM IS THEREFORE LOAD-BEARING AND MUST STAY NON-SUCCESS: this
// decode is a rendering vocabulary, never a permission list. `floor_lane_succeeded` admits
// exactly `LaneSucceeded`, so a result spelling that arrives tomorrow lands in
// `LaneTerminalUnobservable`, carries its own spelling into the receipt, and blocks -- which is
// the same closed-vocabulary law the shell gate enforces with `!= success` rather than with an
// enumeration of bad states.
type FloorLaneTerminal =
    LaneSucceeded
  | LaneFailed
  | LaneCancelled
  | LaneSkipped
  | LaneAbandoned
  | LaneTerminalUnobservable { observed: String }

// WHY EVALUATION STOPPED, AND IT IS TWO AXES RATHER THAN ONE CAUSE FIELD.
//
// THE MEASUREMENT THAT FORCES THIS, and it is a correction to what this module said one revision
// ago rather than an addition to it. A lane's GitHub conclusion does not establish whether the
// SUBJECT was evaluated, and the map is one-to-many in the direction that matters:
//
//   gunbc#9298 build   conclusion FAILURE    subject NOT evaluated -- rustfmt resolved and ran at
//                      admission, then vanished mid-run, so regen never judged the tree. The diff
//                      was one markdown file. Rerun unchanged: PASS.
//   arity-zero import  conclusion FAILURE    subject EVALUATED and refuted -- the parser did its
//                      job and the author has a real defect.
//   gunbc#9269 floor   conclusion CANCELLED  subject NOT evaluated.
//
// `LaneFailed` therefore maps to BOTH subject standings, so no decoding of the conclusion word,
// however careful, can recover it. That is why these are two questions and not two spellings of
// one -- a nickname would be a bijection, and this is not one. Receipts measured by
// warm-hawk-909; the three-layer construction is theirs.
//
// THE DECIDING FACT IS THE PRESENCE OF A TERMINAL SUBJECT RECEIPT, NEVER THE CONCLUSION WORD. A
// typed refusal is NOT automatically a non-evaluation: a parser rejecting a malformed import IS
// the subject being evaluated. Keying on the word `refused` would be wrong in the expensive
// direction.
//
// MECHANISM AND ATTRIBUTION DO NOT COLLAPSE INTO ONE FIELD, and the reason is load-bearing: even
// a CONFIRMED memory kill establishes MECHANISM and still leaves ATTRIBUTION open, because
// intrinsic demand, a mis-sized slot and co-resident host pressure are three different owners
// with three different fixes. Evidence travels in the receipt and must never silently upgrade the
// attribution.
type EvaluationInterruptionKind =
    ToolchainIdentityLost
  | WorkflowCancelled
  | RunnerLost
  | MemoryTermination { detail: String }
  | ProcessSignal { detail: String }

type EvaluationMechanism =
    MechanismEstablished { kind: EvaluationInterruptionKind }
  | MechanismUnestablished

type EvaluationAttribution =
    SubjectIntrinsic
  | EvaluationSubstrate
  | SharedHostCoTenancy
  | SupersededByNewerRun
  | ManuallyCancelled
  | AttributionUnestablished

type EvaluationInterruption {
  mechanism: EvaluationMechanism
  attribution: EvaluationAttribution
  evidence: String
}

// THE RENDERED VERDICT. Exactly one arm does not block, and it is not the green one.
//
// AttemptSuperseded does not block because the head it describes is not a merge candidate: the
// push that superseded this attempt created the head that will be merged, and that head has its
// own run. It is also the only arm that instructs a reader NOT to investigate.
//
// AttemptStandsRed and AttemptStandsUnestablished both block, and the split between them is the
// point of the ruling rather than cosmetic -- BUT IT IS A SPLIT BY CERTAINTY, NOT BY OWNER, and
// an earlier revision of this comment got that wrong in a way worth recording.
//
// It read: "Red means a lane reached a verdict and the verdict was failure -- a subject defect,
// and the author's to fix." THAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and gunbc#9298 above is the counterexample:
// a build lane concluded FAILURE over a one-markdown-file diff because rustfmt vanished mid-run,
// and the subject was never judged at all. So the three states are:
//
//   Unestablished -- no lane produced a conclusion of its own, so the subject verdict is
//                    UNOBSERVABLE from here. Rerun it; it is not a defect in the diff.
//   Red           -- a lane concluded failure. The subject MAY have been evaluated, and this
//                    aggregator cannot tell which, because it holds only the conclusion word.
//                    Go and read the log; do not assume a defect and do not assume infra.
//   Green         -- both lanes succeeded.
//
// THE FIRST ARM SAYS UNOBSERVABLE AND NOT "CERTAINLY NOT EVALUATED", and the one-word difference
// is the same error this comment corrects one arm over. AN ABSENT CONCLUSION IS EVIDENCE ABOUT
// OBSERVABILITY, NEVER ABOUT THE WORLD. The reachable counterexample is ordinary rather than
// exotic: the fold COMPLETES, the verdict is computed, and the job is cancelled during reporting
// or upload -- conclusion `cancelled`, no receipt published, subject evaluated. Measured on two
// of the same night's own cancellations (warm-hawk-909): a floor lane cut at 1m19s inside "Build
// the witness fold" evaluated nothing, and one cut at 2520s with 9000 of 11036 folded had
// evaluated most of its subject. The aggregator cannot distinguish those two, which is the point.
// The remedy is `rerun` either way, so this changes no behaviour -- it is corrected because
// SENTENCES PROPAGATE, and this one will be read by every lane that lands in this arm.
//
// IT ALSO STRENGTHENS THE RECEIPT'S OWN CASE. If an absent conclusion already meant "certainly not
// evaluated", a terminal subject receipt would be buying very little. What it actually buys is the
// CONVERSION OF AN UNOBSERVABLE INTO AN OBSERVATION, which is why
// `floor_subject_receipt_acquisition_trigger` is worth its agreement.
//
// Red is therefore the UNCERTAIN middle and not the certain author-owned end. Both blocking arms
// carry an `EvaluationInterruption` saying exactly how much is known, and on the red arm that is
// honestly `MechanismUnestablished` today. What would make red certain is a TERMINAL SUBJECT
// RECEIPT published by the lane and read here -- that is this pair's next-rung trigger, recorded
// on `floor_subject_receipt_acquisition_trigger`, and it is a different acquisition from the head
// one.
type FloorRenderedVerdict =
    AttemptSuperseded { measured: String, current: String }
  | AttemptStandsGreen { standing: FloorHeadStanding }
  | AttemptStandsRed {
      standing: FloorHeadStanding,
      build: FloorLaneTerminal,
      floor: FloorLaneTerminal,
      interruption: EvaluationInterruption
    }
  | AttemptStandsUnestablished {
      standing: FloorHeadStanding,
      build: FloorLaneTerminal,
      floor: FloorLaneTerminal,
      interruption: EvaluationInterruption
    }

// THE WIRE SPELLINGS, DECLARED ONCE HERE AND CONSUMED BY BOTH REALIZATIONS.
//
// The shell gate branches on these literals and this module decodes them, so they are one
// authority rather than two spellings that happen to agree today. `gunbc.required_lanes_gate`
// imports them; a witness over the emitted yaml asserts the rendered text carries exactly these.
data floor_lane_result_success: String = "success"
data floor_lane_result_failure: String = "failure"
data floor_lane_result_cancelled: String = "cancelled"
data floor_lane_result_skipped: String = "skipped"
data floor_lane_result_abandoned: String = "abandoned"

fn floor_lane_terminal_from_result(result: String) -> FloorLaneTerminal {
  if result == floor_lane_result_success {
    LaneSucceeded
  } else {
    if result == floor_lane_result_failure {
      LaneFailed
    } else {
      if result == floor_lane_result_cancelled {
        LaneCancelled
      } else {
        if result == floor_lane_result_skipped {
          LaneSkipped
        } else {
          if result == floor_lane_result_abandoned {
            LaneAbandoned
          } else {
            LaneTerminalUnobservable { observed: result }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn floor_lane_terminal_name(terminal: FloorLaneTerminal) -> String {
  match terminal {
    LaneSucceeded => floor_lane_result_success
    LaneFailed => floor_lane_result_failure
    LaneCancelled => floor_lane_result_cancelled
    LaneSkipped => floor_lane_result_skipped
    LaneAbandoned => floor_lane_result_abandoned
    LaneTerminalUnobservable { observed: observed } => observed
  }
}

fn floor_lane_succeeded(terminal: FloorLaneTerminal) -> Bool {
  match terminal {
    LaneSucceeded => true
    LaneFailed => false
    LaneCancelled => false
    LaneSkipped => false
    LaneAbandoned => false
    LaneTerminalUnobservable { observed: _ } => false
  }
}

fn floor_lane_failed(terminal: FloorLaneTerminal) -> Bool {
  match terminal {
    LaneSucceeded => false
    LaneFailed => true
    LaneCancelled => false
    LaneSkipped => false
    LaneAbandoned => false
    LaneTerminalUnobservable { observed: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE HEAD-STANDING WIRE SPELLINGS. `not-applicable` and `unobserved` are two states and not one:
// collapsing them would put "this attempt has no pull request" and "we could not reach the pull
// request" under one word, and only the second is a deficit anyone should act on.
data floor_head_standing_current: String = "current"
data floor_head_standing_superseded: String = "superseded"
data floor_head_standing_not_applicable: String = "not-applicable"
data floor_head_standing_unobserved: String = "unobserved"

fn floor_head_standing_name(standing: FloorHeadStanding) -> String {
  match standing {
    FloorHeadCurrent { measured: _ } => floor_head_standing_current
    FloorHeadSuperseded { measured: _, current: _ } => floor_head_standing_superseded
    FloorHeadUnobservable { measured: _, cause: cause } => match cause {
      NoPullRequestSubject => floor_head_standing_not_applicable
      CurrentHeadNotAcquired { detail: _ } => floor_head_standing_unobserved
    }
  }
}

// THE COMPOSITION, AND ITS ONE ASYMMETRY.
//
// Supersession short-circuits the lane axis entirely -- an abandoned head's lane results are not
// evidence about anything, whatever they say. Every other head state falls through to the lane
// axis unchanged, INCLUDING `CurrentHeadNotAcquired`: an unacquired head axis is treated exactly
// as a current one, which is the fail-closed arm and preserves the gate's BLOCKING PREDICATE
// exactly as it stood before this module existed: block iff either lane is not `success`. The
// emitted script is not byte-identical and the annotation text is deliberately different -- what
// is unchanged is which runs block, not what they print. That is the property that makes the head axis safe to add before
// its acquisition is built: with the acquisition absent, every run takes the unobserved arm and
// nothing about the verdict changes.
fn floor_rendered_verdict(
  standing: FloorHeadStanding,
  build: FloorLaneTerminal,
  floor: FloorLaneTerminal
) -> FloorRenderedVerdict {
  match standing {
    FloorHeadSuperseded { measured: measured, current: current } =>
      AttemptSuperseded { measured: measured, current: current }
    FloorHeadCurrent { measured: _ } =>
      floor_standing_attempt_verdict(standing: standing, build: build, floor: floor)
    FloorHeadUnobservable { measured: _, cause: _ } =>
      floor_standing_attempt_verdict(standing: standing, build: build, floor: floor)
  }
}

fn floor_standing_attempt_verdict(
  standing: FloorHeadStanding,
  build: FloorLaneTerminal,
  floor: FloorLaneTerminal
) -> FloorRenderedVerdict {
  if floor_lane_succeeded(terminal: build) && floor_lane_succeeded(terminal: floor) {
    AttemptStandsGreen { standing: standing }
  } else {
    if floor_lane_failed(terminal: build) || floor_lane_failed(terminal: floor) {
      AttemptStandsRed {
        standing: standing,
        build: build,
        floor: floor,
        interruption: floor_attempt_interruption(build: build, floor: floor)
      }
    } else {
      AttemptStandsUnestablished {
        standing: standing,
        build: build,
        floor: floor,
        interruption: floor_attempt_interruption(build: build, floor: floor)
      }
    }
  }
}

// WHAT THE AGGREGATOR CAN DERIVE FROM WHAT IT HOLDS, AND NOTHING MORE.
//
// The aggregator reads two `needs.*.result` words. That is enough to establish the MECHANISM in
// exactly one case -- a lane the workflow cancelled -- and enough to establish the ATTRIBUTION in
// none, because a cancellation says only that this attempt stopped and never why. Every other
// spelling leaves both axes open, and this function says so rather than guessing.
//
// THE CHEAP ARM IS DELIBERATE AND THE EXPENSIVE ONE IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Populating mechanism
// after a silent death needs an observer OUTSIDE the child's kill domain plus a job to slot to
// cgroup join, which reaches the runner allocation authority. If that landed as one unit with
// this, the correct cheap half would wait on it and nobody would get either.
//
// THE HAZARD THIS FUNCTION CREATES, NAMED SO IT IS NOT DISCOVERED LATER: `MechanismUnestablished`
// is honest the day it is written and indistinguishable from an untyped infra bucket a week
// later. It is rendered into the aggregator's annotation on every blocking run precisely so its
// population stays countable from the runs that produce it -- a state nobody measures is a state
// that never gets promoted. The instrument is the required run's own log line, named rather than
// transcribed.
fn floor_lane_interruption(terminal: FloorLaneTerminal) -> EvaluationInterruption {
  match terminal {
    LaneCancelled => EvaluationInterruption {
      mechanism: MechanismEstablished { kind: WorkflowCancelled },
      attribution: AttributionUnestablished,
      evidence: floor_lane_terminal_name(terminal: terminal)
    }
    LaneSucceeded => floor_unestablished_interruption(terminal: terminal)
    LaneFailed => floor_unestablished_interruption(terminal: terminal)
    LaneSkipped => floor_unestablished_interruption(terminal: terminal)
    LaneAbandoned => floor_unestablished_interruption(terminal: terminal)
    LaneTerminalUnobservable { observed: _ } => floor_unestablished_interruption(terminal: terminal)
  }
}

fn floor_unestablished_interruption(terminal: FloorLaneTerminal) -> EvaluationInterruption {
  EvaluationInterruption {
    mechanism: MechanismUnestablished,
    attribution: AttributionUnestablished,
    evidence: floor_lane_terminal_name(terminal: terminal)
  }
}

// THE INTERRUPTION IS THE NON-SUCCEEDING LANE'S, and the build lane is read first only because
// something must be. When both lanes are non-success the receipt carries both spellings anyway,
// on the verdict's own `build` and `floor` fields, so nothing is lost by the choice.
fn floor_attempt_interruption(
  build: FloorLaneTerminal,
  floor: FloorLaneTerminal
) -> EvaluationInterruption {
  if floor_lane_succeeded(terminal: build) {
    floor_lane_interruption(terminal: floor)
  } else {
    floor_lane_interruption(terminal: build)
  }
}

data floor_mechanism_unestablished_name: String = "unestablished"
data floor_mechanism_workflow_cancelled_name: String = "workflow-cancelled"
data floor_mechanism_toolchain_identity_lost_name: String = "toolchain-identity-lost"
data floor_mechanism_runner_lost_name: String = "runner-lost"
data floor_mechanism_memory_termination_name: String = "memory-termination"
data floor_mechanism_process_signal_name: String = "process-signal"

fn floor_evaluation_mechanism_name(mechanism: EvaluationMechanism) -> String {
  match mechanism {
    MechanismUnestablished => floor_mechanism_unestablished_name
    MechanismEstablished { kind: kind } => match kind {
      WorkflowCancelled => floor_mechanism_workflow_cancelled_name
      ToolchainIdentityLost => floor_mechanism_toolchain_identity_lost_name
      RunnerLost => floor_mechanism_runner_lost_name
      MemoryTermination { detail: _ } => floor_mechanism_memory_termination_name
      ProcessSignal { detail: _ } => floor_mechanism_process_signal_name
    }
  }
}

data floor_attribution_unestablished_name: String = "unestablished"
data floor_attribution_subject_intrinsic_name: String = "subject-intrinsic"
data floor_attribution_evaluation_substrate_name: String = "evaluation-substrate"
data floor_attribution_shared_host_co_tenancy_name: String = "shared-host-co-tenancy"
data floor_attribution_superseded_by_newer_run_name: String = "superseded-by-newer-run"
data floor_attribution_manually_cancelled_name: String = "manually-cancelled"

fn floor_evaluation_attribution_name(attribution: EvaluationAttribution) -> String {
  match attribution {
    AttributionUnestablished => floor_attribution_unestablished_name
    SubjectIntrinsic => floor_attribution_subject_intrinsic_name
    EvaluationSubstrate => floor_attribution_evaluation_substrate_name
    SharedHostCoTenancy => floor_attribution_shared_host_co_tenancy_name
    SupersededByNewerRun => floor_attribution_superseded_by_newer_run_name
    ManuallyCancelled => floor_attribution_manually_cancelled_name
  }
}

// WHAT THE REQUIRED CONTEXT DOES WITH THE VERDICT. Three of four arms block, and the one that
// does not is the one whose head will never be merged.
fn floor_rendered_verdict_blocks(verdict: FloorRenderedVerdict) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    AttemptSuperseded { measured: _, current: _ } => false
    AttemptStandsGreen { standing: _ } => false
    AttemptStandsRed { standing: _, build: _, floor: _, interruption: _ } => true
    AttemptStandsUnestablished { standing: _, build: _, floor: _, interruption: _ } => true
  }
}

data floor_rendered_verdict_superseded_name: String = "superseded-attempt"
data floor_rendered_verdict_green_name: String = "stands-green"
data floor_rendered_verdict_red_name: String = "stands-red"
data floor_rendered_verdict_unestablished_name: String = "stands-unestablished"

fn floor_rendered_verdict_name(verdict: FloorRenderedVerdict) -> String {
  match verdict {
    AttemptSuperseded { measured: _, current: _ } => floor_rendered_verdict_superseded_name
    AttemptStandsGreen { standing: _ } => floor_rendered_verdict_green_name
    AttemptStandsRed { standing: _, build: _, floor: _, interruption: _ } =>
      floor_rendered_verdict_red_name
    AttemptStandsUnestablished { standing: _, build: _, floor: _, interruption: _ } =>
      floor_rendered_verdict_unestablished_name
  }
}

// WHAT THE HEAD AXIS IS WAITING ON, NAMED SO THE STALL IS TRACKED RATHER THAN SILENT (DESIGN
// 4b(2)).
//
// `FloorHeadSuperseded` is unreachable from production input today, and that is a property of the
// ACQUISITION rather than of this composition: nothing in the required run reads the pull
// request's current head, so no two observations can disagree. The census behind that statement:
// no step in any emitted workflow shells to `gh api`, `gh run` or `gh pr` or curls the API; the
// only run-metadata expression anywhere is `github.run_id` inside the witnesses concurrency key,
// which is a key string and not an observation; and `witnesses.yml` grants `contents: read` with
// no token in any job's env, so no such step could authenticate if it existed.
//
// THE SHAPE IS NOT NET-NEW, ONLY ITS ACQUISITION HERE IS. `extdeps.github.pulls` declares
// `github.Pulls.Get` over a rest transport returning `PullRequest`, whose `head` carries `sha`,
// and it has executing consumers in `gunbc.tools.review` and `gunbc.tools.review_codex`. What is
// missing is a step inside the required run that calls it, and the permission grant that would
// let it.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: an operator agreement enrolling one authenticated read of the pull request's
// current head in the aggregator, with `pull-requests: read` on that job. When it lands,
// `FloorHeadSuperseded` becomes reachable from production, `required_lanes_gate` emits the
// superseded arm, and `w_RED_no_emitted_gate_arm_reaches_superseded` goes red -- deliberately, so
// the day the acquisition arrives is the day someone is forced back to this paragraph.
data floor_attempt_standing_acquisition_trigger: String = "Head-standing acquisition is not enrolled in the required run. FloorHeadSuperseded is reachable from a fixture and not from production. Retire this row when an operator-agreed authenticated read of the pull request current head, with pull-requests read on the aggregator job, lands in gunbc.required_lanes_gate."

// THE SECOND ACQUISITION, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT ONE FROM THE HEAD (DESIGN 4b(2)).
//
// BOTH BLOCKING ARMS ARE WAITING ON ONE THING, and it is the same thing. `AttemptStandsRed` is
// the uncertain middle: a lane concluded failure, and whether the SUBJECT was evaluated is not
// recoverable from that word. `AttemptStandsUnestablished` is the unobservable end: no conclusion
// was produced, and whether the subject was evaluated before the job stopped is not recoverable
// either. What would decide BOTH is a TERMINAL SUBJECT RECEIPT -- the lane publishing, as a job
// output the aggregator can read, whether its own evaluation reached a verdict.
//
// THAT IS WHAT THE RECEIPT BUYS, STATED AT ITS REAL WEIGHT: it converts an UNOBSERVABLE into an
// OBSERVATION. With it present, red splits into "subject evaluated and refuted" (the author's) and
// "subject never evaluated" (a rerun); unestablished stops being a single word covering both a
// lane cut before it began and a lane cut after it had folded 9000 of 11036 claims; and
// `MechanismUnestablished` stops being the destination for every failure whose cause was the
// substrate.
//
// THIS IS NOT THE HEAD ACQUISITION AND MUST NOT BE BUNDLED WITH IT. The head acquisition is one
// authenticated read of a pull request; this is a lane publishing a receipt about its own run.
// They need different agreements, they fail differently, and pairing them would make each wait
// on the other.
//
// AND IT IS NOT THE SUPERVISOR EITHER. Establishing MECHANISM after a silent death -- a memory
// kill, a lost runner -- needs an observer outside the child's kill domain and a job to slot to
// cgroup join, which reaches the runner allocation authority. That is a third, larger trigger,
// and the cheap correct half above must not wait on it.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a terminal subject receipt published by each required lane and read by the
// aggregator. When it lands, `w_RED_a_failed_lane_carries_no_established_mechanism` goes red --
// deliberately, so nobody can land the receipt without being routed back here.
data floor_subject_receipt_acquisition_trigger: String = "A lane's GitHub conclusion does not establish whether its subject was evaluated; gunbc#9298 concluded FAILURE over a one-file markdown diff with the subject never judged. Retire this row when each required lane publishes a terminal subject receipt the aggregator reads, at which point AttemptStandsRed splits into subject-refuted and subject-not-evaluated."
